1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,040
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome to this brand new series on pediatric dentistry.

2
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:15,420
 Now pediatric dentistry is multidisciplinary in nature and it encompasses a wide range

3
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:17,720
 of the other specialties.

4
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:22,360
 So these videos, if you're watching them after some of my other series, may provide

5
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:27,560
 some helpful overlap and review with some of the other sections.

6
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:33,280
 Now with that in mind, pediatric dentistry is certainly not the same as adult dentistry

7
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:39,080
 because kids and primary teeth present some unique challenges and we need to talk about

8
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,000
 what those are.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,520
 So we're going to cover a lot of great topics in this series and it's definitely one of

10
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:49,400
 the most requested as of recently.

11
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:55,520
 The board examiners combine orthodontics and pediatric dentistry into one category and

12
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:00,480
 their guidelines but I think it's honestly more helpful to focus on each one of those

13
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:06,120
 individually so that's why I'm going to have a separate series for each of them.

14
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:11,020
 So that being said, like all my videos, I'm going to focus only on the highest yield things

15
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:13,200
 that you need to know for the board exam.

16
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,240
 My hope is that these videos will help you prepare for it and also give you a nice overview

17
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:23,280
 for clinical application and general knowledge.

18
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:29,660
 So with that, let's get started and talk about odontogenesis or tooth development.

19
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:35,640
 And this is honestly a very complex topic but I'm excited to boil it down in the most

20
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:38,800
 simplified way possible for you.

21
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:44,840
 Now tooth development is dependent on both genetic control and also environmental factors

22
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:51,960
 like nutrition, trauma, infection, or even excess fluoride intake during tooth formation

23
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:56,880
 and we'll talk more about these environmental factors and developmental disturbances and

24
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:01,360
 anomalies in the following video.

25
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:07,040
 So this is a really nice schematic showing the different developing layers and the different

26
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:11,080
 stages from beginning to erupted tooth.

27
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:15,680
 Now we're going to talk about all of these in detail and I'm definitely going to focus

28
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:22,040
 on the bud, the cap, and the bell stages which are nice to remember because each of those

29
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:29,040
 is corresponding to the shape that the enamel organ is assuming in each one of those stages.

30
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:37,840
 So let's talk about each one in detail starting with thickening or initiation.

31
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:44,280
 So initiation starts six weeks in utero and this is referring to how long the fetus has

32
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:47,160
 been developing in the womb.

33
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,200
 So there are a couple of layers that we have to address here.

34
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:58,360
 The oral epithelium is the outer layer of the developing mouth and this has both an upper

35
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:07,360
 and a lower arch form that referring to the upper and the lower part of the eventual and

36
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:09,880
 ultimate mouth.

37
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:17,800
 The dental lamina is a thickening of this oral epithelium and the first evidence of

38
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,960
 a forming or budding tooth.

39
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:26,840
 Now this image is a two-dimensional cross section so imagine that the thickened area

40
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:34,360
 is going both into the screen and coming out of the screen to create a whole row of thickened

41
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:40,920
 oral epithelium where individual teeth will eventually pinch off from.

42
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:48,320
 The ectomecin chime is this thick underlying purple layer and that signals the overlying

43
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:54,800
 oral epithelium to proliferate into the dental lamina.

44
00:03:54,800 --> 00:04:00,880
 Now the oral epithelium and the dental lamina are both derived from the ectoderm.

45
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:07,280
 The ectomecin chime is also derived from the ectoderm but more specifically the neural crest.

46
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:13,600
 It's called the ectomecin chime because it's derived from the ectoderm indirectly but it

47
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:20,200
 develops into tissue that is physiologically similar to mesenchymal tissue or mesoderm

48
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:22,840
 derived tissue.

49
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:28,760
 Failure in this very first step can lead to major issues either the tooth is completely

50
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:34,200
 missing or we get an extra tooth.

51
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:41,360
 The next stage is the bud stage and this begins eight weeks in utero so two weeks later.

52
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:51,000
 Here the dental lamina is quickly growing and invades into the underlying mesenchym.

53
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,800
 So these proliferating areas of the dental lamina are pinching off to produce these buds

54
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:04,240
 and these buds are called dental placodes.

55
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:05,720
 Now this is really important.

56
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:14,440
 All primary teeth and permanent molars arise directly from this dental lamina.

57
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:24,080
 So they look exactly as the schematic is showing here but permanent incisors, canines and premolars

58
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:26,880
 arise from their primary predecessor.

59
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:28,320
 So what does that mean?

60
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:34,120
 Well a secondary bud pinches off from the first bud so imagine there's a separate smaller

61
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:42,720
 bud coming off right around there and that goes through the same stages from this point

62
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:48,400
 forward to develop the permanent teeth except for the molars.

63
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:54,560
 Now in this make sense because these are all succedaneous teeth or teeth that eventually

64
00:05:54,560 --> 00:06:01,560
 replace a primary tooth whereas all of the primary teeth and the permanent molars have

65
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,480
 no teeth that they're replacing.

66
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:07,800
 They're the first ones in their respective regions to come into the mouth.

67
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:15,520
 So I find that that's the best way to remember this concept and to keep that information straight.

68
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:22,700
 Now additionally the mesenchym is also beginning to condense underneath the bud and that's

69
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:30,120
 what these dark blue, dark purple dots are showing that this tissue is starting to condense

70
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,920
 directly underneath that bud.

71
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:38,660
 Here in the bud stage again results in congenitally missing teeth where the budding process doesn't

72
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:47,560
 occur at all or supernumerate teeth where we have excessive budding happening.

73
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:53,240
 Alright next we have the cap stage and this happens 9 weeks in utero.

74
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:59,280
 So the cap stage is also known as proliferation and it's when the bud continues to grow and

75
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:05,160
 proliferate while also differentiating into some new layers.

76
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:13,440
 Now each dental plaque code shown here grows and indents to form the enamel organ.

77
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:17,680
 So this is now called the enamel organ.

78
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:22,040
 And the enamel organ will ultimately create the enamel.

79
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:25,560
 Now we're not anywhere close to that stage yet but just keep that in the back of your

80
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:26,960
 head.

81
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:33,360
 The enamel organ consists of various very important layers to remember.

82
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:38,560
 The first is the OEE or outer enamel epithelium.

83
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:45,000
 And this is the outer cell layer so it's what I'm highlighting right here.

84
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,520
 I'll try to get these in a couple different colors for you.

85
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:55,520
 The next layer is the inner enamel epithelium and that is the inner cell layer so that's

86
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:01,320
 going to be this part right there.

87
00:08:01,320 --> 00:08:10,600
 And next we have the stelate reticulum which are the cells between the OEE and the IEE.

88
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:16,520
 So that's all of this stuff all in here.

89
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:23,800
 And finally we have the enamel knot which are these little areas of focal thickening.

90
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:32,320
 I kind of covered it up but there are these points or peaks along this inner enamel epithelium

91
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:36,600
 that eventually determine where the cusp tips will be.

92
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:42,520
 And they're also the signaling centers for the developing tooth.

93
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:49,520
 Now that condensed mesenchyme that we were talking about before aggregates and now it

94
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:55,960
 finally forms its own entity and it's called the dental papilla.

95
00:08:55,960 --> 00:09:03,720
 So essentially the dental papilla is now wearing this enamel organ as a sort of cap.

96
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:10,400
 The dental papilla will ultimately create and become the dentin and the pulp.

97
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:16,720
 Again as with the other things in italicized font here that's just an ultimate goal but

98
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:22,880
 we're not anywhere close to that point yet in the tooth development process.

99
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:29,440
 So the dental follicle is the sac that surrounds the enamel organ and the dental papilla.

100
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:37,040
 So you can think of it like this black outline that encircles this entire enamel organ and

101
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:40,560
 underlying dental papilla.

102
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:46,660
 So again the dental papilla is wearing the enamel organ as its cap and the dental follicle

103
00:09:46,660 --> 00:09:49,080
 surrounds it all.

104
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:58,520
 Failure in this process could result in congenitally missing or supernumerate teeth if it occurs

105
00:09:58,520 --> 00:10:06,320
 early on but more likely you're going to see a cyst, odontoma, germination or fusion or

106
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:14,280
 dentindente depending on the amount of cell differentiation that has occurred.

107
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:20,160
 Alright next we have the bell stage and this one begins 11 weeks in utero and there are

108
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:25,160
 two components or two phases in the bell stage.

109
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:32,320
 The first is called histodifferentiation, histo meaning tissue and this is where we get

110
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:38,680
 differentiation and transformation of those layers we were talking about into distinct

111
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:48,040
 cell types that will be responsible for secreting our final tooth tissue hence the name here.

112
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:57,880
 So the cells of the inner enamel epithelium differentiate into ameloblasts whereas the

113
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:05,440
 cells of the dental papilla differentiate into odontoblasts all of which are tall and

114
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:07,600
 columnar in shape.

115
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:11,320
 Now I matched the colors here it might be a little bit hard to see I apologize they're

116
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:19,200
 very thin but that darker tan layer is the ameloblasts which were part of the IEE and

117
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:25,800
 then this part that kind of burgundy color thin layer are the odontoblasts that outer

118
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:31,020
 portion of the dental papilla right up against that IEE.

119
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:37,160
 So that junction right there is going to be the eventual dentin enamel junction, the

120
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:41,680
 DEJ of that tooth.

121
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:47,120
 Now this makes sense because ameloblasts are secreting enamel and the odontoblasts are

122
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:53,400
 going to be secreting dentin which is consistent with what we saw in the previous slide.

123
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:59,400
 Now defects in this process of histodifferentiation can cause amelogenesis imperfecta where you

124
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:06,320
 get abnormal enamel formation you get this yellow brown discoloration or dentinogenesis

125
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:12,640
 imperfecta where you get abnormal dentin formation or this brown greatest coloration and again

126
00:12:12,640 --> 00:12:17,000
 this all makes sense because of what is happening during this stage.

127
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:22,280
 So hopefully you can draw those connections and really help to lock this into your brain.

128
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:30,040
 The second phase is called morpho differentiation, morpho meaning form, structure, shape.

129
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:36,600
 This is where we determine the size and the shape of the eventual tooth crown.

130
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:41,920
 Now of course this is also happening simultaneously with histodifferentiation hence why I have the

131
00:12:41,920 --> 00:12:47,880
 same time period of 11 weeks in utero for the bell stage.

132
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:53,680
 So it makes sense that a failure in a stage that's all about determining shape and size

133
00:12:53,680 --> 00:13:01,240
 are going to have shape and size abnormalities like peg laterals, macrodontia, and a couple

134
00:13:01,240 --> 00:13:06,240
 more that we'll talk about in our next video.

135
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:08,800
 So next we have apposition.

136
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:15,080
 Now an apposition happening 14 weeks in utero, first we're going to have those odontoblasts

137
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:24,040
 secreting a dentin matrix of collagen which then signals the amule blasts to secrete an

138
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:28,020
 enamel matrix of amuleogenin.

139
00:13:28,020 --> 00:13:34,120
 Then after the enamel starts being produced the cervical loop jumps into the action and

140
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:37,840
 root development starts to take place.

141
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:43,480
 Now the cervical loop is where the IEE and the OEE layers meet.

142
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:51,720
 So that's going to be right at this point here where that outer enamel epithelium and

143
00:13:51,720 --> 00:13:57,940
 that inner enamel epithelium now amule blasts joins together and here's the point on this

144
00:13:57,940 --> 00:13:58,940
 side here.

145
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:08,000
 So our cervical loop going circumferentially around the developing tooth bell.

146
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,800
 So this part, the cervical loop elongates and grows down.

147
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:16,720
 So it's growing down in this direction.

148
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:22,160
 And it forms an extension called hertz epithelial root sheath.

149
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:25,080
 We'll just call it hertz for short.

150
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:32,040
 And this stimulates odontoblasts to secrete radicular dentin which is dentin as part of

151
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:34,080
 the root.

152
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:40,200
 Now hertz eventually disintegrates leaving behind little clusters of cells called the

153
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:47,800
 epithelial rests of ballastae that stay present in the ultimate periodontal ligament.

154
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:56,480
 It's just a vestigial structure as part of the developing tooth.

155
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:03,680
 Now the stellate reticulum that's this part here between the IEE and the OEE collapses

156
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:10,800
 and deflates kind of like a popped balloon and the outer and inner enamel epithelium meet

157
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:17,800
 together and lay along the top of the enamel matrix that's beginning to form.

158
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:27,080
 And the combination of the deflated IEE and OEE becomes the reduced enamel epithelium.

159
00:15:27,080 --> 00:15:33,400
 And that kind of protects the tooth as it erupts and eventually it'll form the junctional

160
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:38,920
 epithelium part of the gingiva.

161
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:45,480
 And then what remains of the dental papilla will eventually become the pulp.

162
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:51,960
 Now disturbances in this step where we're getting a position or initial laying down of

163
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:58,320
 these matrices, you could get like trauma in the stage.

164
00:15:58,320 --> 00:16:03,800
 If you get trauma to a primary tooth, you could disrupt the cells while they're laying

165
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:06,520
 down these initial matrix layers.

166
00:16:06,520 --> 00:16:12,360
 So say you have the primary teeth are erupted in the mouth and those permanent teeth are

167
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:17,360
 currently going through a position at those secondary buds.

168
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:22,200
 And if you knock a primary tooth, you can actually disrupt those ameloblasts, let's say, cause

169
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:24,160
 some ameloblasts injury.

170
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:31,160
 And this can result in localized areas of enamel hypoplasia called Turner's hypoplasia.

171
00:16:31,160 --> 00:16:39,640
 And again, we'll talk about that more in the next video if you're curious about that.

172
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:47,280
 Alright, and we're almost done here, maturation is also known as calcification or mineralization.

173
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:53,120
 And this one takes place at various time points for different teeth.

174
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:59,400
 But important to know, this is by far the longest stage of tooth development and it involves

175
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:04,960
 finally the deposition of the ultimate enamel and dentin.

176
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:09,440
 We're in opposition, we're just kind of laying down those initial matrices, and now we're

177
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:15,320
 talking about transforming those into the final tooth structure.

178
00:17:15,320 --> 00:17:21,760
 So the calcification process begins at the cusp tips and incisal edges where those enamel

179
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:26,400
 knots were and proceeds in a cervical direction.

180
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:33,000
 Now it takes two years for a primary tooth crown on average and four to five years on

181
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:38,280
 average for a permanent tooth crown to complete this maturation process.

182
00:17:38,280 --> 00:17:41,800
 And that's not even including the root, it takes quite a few more years for the root

183
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:42,800
 to finish developing.

184
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:46,400
 So this is a really, really long process.

185
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:53,560
 And now that explains why these disturbances, these defects rather, are so common because

186
00:17:53,560 --> 00:17:56,240
 the critical time period is much longer here.

187
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:59,800
 There's a lot more time for something to go wrong.

188
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:06,780
 So disturbances like localized infection and trauma can result in hypocalcification or

189
00:18:06,780 --> 00:18:10,080
 hypomineralization, those are those white spots.

190
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:16,440
 You can get fluoresis if you have excessive systemic fluoride ingestion.

191
00:18:16,440 --> 00:18:22,200
 And fluoride ingestion greater than one PBM can affect ameloblasts.

192
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:28,400
 So it causes a defective enamel matrix and a modelled enamel appearance.

193
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:35,360
 Now this is going to be affecting children from their second trimester, a week 14 in

194
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:38,480
 utero, through eight years old.

195
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:44,300
 And that's the period while the teeth are forming 16 years if you count the wisdom teeth.

196
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:47,520
 Now adults are not affected.

197
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:50,560
 Tetracycline staining is kind of similar.

198
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:56,800
 Tetracycline taken as an antibiotic can also cause intrinsic staining.

199
00:18:56,800 --> 00:19:04,280
 And it's also going to affect those same children from second trimester or week 14 through

200
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:07,420
 eight years old while the teeth are forming.

201
00:19:07,420 --> 00:19:11,840
 But what's different here is that tetracycline doesn't mess up the enamel.

202
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:18,960
 It binds to calcium and gets incorporated into the hydroxyapatite crystals in the dentin

203
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:20,400
 layer.

204
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:27,360
 So fluoresis messes up enamel, tetracycline stains the dentin.

205
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:32,160
 And something very important to think about all this stuff with the defects is that the

206
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:42,760
 later the disturbance occurs along this development process, the more minor the defect is, right?

207
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:47,200
 We were talking about before with thickening and the bud stage if something went wrong,

208
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:51,800
 we might just not have a tooth at all or we might end up with an extra tooth or teeth.

209
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:55,240
 But if it happens a little bit later, well, we might get lucky and just have a little

210
00:19:55,240 --> 00:19:56,760
 white spot on there.

211
00:19:56,760 --> 00:20:03,800
 So it depends on when it occurs, how major that ultimate effect will be.

212
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:10,040
 So a summary, we have our tooth germ, which includes the enamel organ, dental papilla,

213
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:17,960
 dental follicle, follicle, and here are all the cells and cell layers and the materials

214
00:20:17,960 --> 00:20:23,200
 that are eventually produced by the cells of these certain layers.

215
00:20:23,200 --> 00:20:27,240
 So that will be an awesome reference for you.

216
00:20:27,240 --> 00:20:33,560
 And I really hope this has helped you kind of understand the complex, but also really

217
00:20:33,560 --> 00:20:38,520
 cool atontogenesis process.

218
00:20:38,520 --> 00:20:44,720
 Now for some other important dates to know, these are approximate calcification dates

219
00:20:44,720 --> 00:20:47,360
 for the primary teeth.

220
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:53,840
 And I've arranged them ideally for the best and easiest memorization for you.

221
00:20:53,840 --> 00:21:00,000
 And also to know here, the calcification of primary tooth roots is normally completed

222
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,840
 at around three to four years of age.

223
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:08,120
 This is talking about the crowns and when they're starting to calcify.

224
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:14,000
 Now here's a handy memorization tool that'll really help you remember this info.

225
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:22,840
 The central incisors kind of using ortho-language here, they're the As we call them, the lateral

